      "math_academy_id": "e-1445",
      "problem": "Identify the equation of the graph shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218419/assets/36a2a45f6078cdf04b798c39b6382c1f40411375a3f16ef77e996b39aa0009b3.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $2$ units *left*, so we have $h=-2.$ The parabola is also translated $1$ unit up, so we have $k=1.$ We can also see that the vertex is at $(-2,1),$ so again we must have $h=-2$ and $k=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the graph is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-(-2))^{2}+1 \\\\ y & =(x+2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
